var lunrIndex = [{"id":"changelog","title":"Changelog","body":"All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https:\/\/keepachangelog.com\/en\/1.0.0\/),\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https:\/\/semver.org\/spec\/v2.0.0.html).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Fixed\n\n- Works with symfony\/console 7. Commands set their names in `configure()` instead of the static `$defaultName` property, which Symfony Console 7 removed; on 7.x every command failed with \"cannot have an empty name\".\n- Patterns now match files reached through a symlinked directory. A directory reachable by two paths was only ever listed under one of them, so a glob such as `web\/*\/custom\/*\/bin\/*` could not match a theme symlinked out of an install directory, and an explicit path had to be listed instead. The missing paths are derived from the single directory walk, so no extra filesystem scanning is done.\n- Where several paths address one inode, by symlink or hard link, the permission applied is the one that won during calculation. Previously whichever path sorted first alphabetically decided the outcome, so a later instruction could be silently dropped.\n- `optimize` keeps a path that currently matches nothing. Such a pattern was treated as covered by any glob in the same section and removed, discarding configuration written for paths that do not exist yet--build artifacts, or files created by an install step. Genuine redundancy is still consolidated.\n- `HandleMemory` compares the memory limit in bytes. As strings `\"1G\"` sorts below `\"512M\"`, so an existing limit of `1G`, `2G` or `-1` (unlimited) was being reduced to 512M, while a limit of `64M` was left too low.\n\n### Changed\n\n- Multiple configuration files are now applied sequentially: each file is processed only after the previous one has been fully processed. A later file still supersedes an earlier one for any path both target.\n- As a result, an override to `file_permissions` or `directory_permissions` is no longer retroactive; it applies to the paths listed in the file that declares it and in any file processed after it. Previously the last override found in any file was applied to every path.\n\n## [0.0.24] - 2026-07-20\n\n### Added\n\n- Added `optimize` command to consolidate configuration paths, support glob patterns, and create backups.\n\n### Changed\n\n- Updated `apply-perms.sh` with `--no-dev` and `--help` options, and improved production environment detection.\n- Refined configuration loading to support path consolidation.\n\n## [0.0.23] - 2026-07-20\n\n### Changed\n\n- Updated documentation build process and refined PHPUnit configuration.\n\n### Removed\n\n- Removed unused scripts: `bin\/bind_book.sh` and `bin\/flush_book.sh`.\n\n## [0.0.22] - 2026-07-17\n\n### Removed\n\n- **BREAKING CHANGE**: The `writeable` configuration key has been removed in favor of the canonical `writable`. Using `writeable` in any configuration file will now throw a `RuntimeException`.\n\n## [0.0.21] - 2026-07-17\n\n### Fixed\n\n- Fixed a bug where configuration defaults were incorrectly merged into every file load, causing them to supersede actual settings when multiple files (e.g., `easy-perms.yml` and `easy-perms.dev.yml`) were used.\n- Improved octal value handling in configuration merging to ensure consistency when YAML parsers interpret permissions as integers."},{"id":"optimize","title":"Config Optimization","body":"The `optimize` command is used to clean up and organize your configuration files. It performs several operations to ensure your configuration remains concise and manageable.\n\n## Key Features\n\n- **Consolidation**: It removes redundant explicit paths that are already covered by glob patterns, favoring the more general glob patterns.\n- **Sorting**: It sorts paths alphabetically within each group (e.g., `writable`, `readonly`), ignoring single quotes during the sorting process for consistent results.\n- **Backups**: Before making any changes, it creates a backup of your original configuration file with a timestamp suffix.\n\n## Scope\n\nEach configuration file is rewritten from its own contents alone, so:\n\n- **The order you list the files in does not matter.** This is unlike `apply`, where a later file supersedes an earlier one.\n- A glob in one file never removes a path from another file, and a glob in one section never removes a path from another section. Coverage is only ever considered within a single section of a single file.\n- A path that matches nothing at the moment is left alone, because there is no evidence a glob covers it. This keeps configuration for paths that do not exist yet, such as build artifacts or files an install step creates.\n\nConsolidation reflects the files on disk when you run it. If a glob covers an explicit path today, that path is removed; should the filesystem later change so the glob no longer matches, the permission goes with it. Re-run `apply` after optimizing to confirm the result is what you expect.\n\n## Usage\n\nYou can run the optimization as often as you like:\n\n```bash\neasy-perms optimize easy-perms.yml\n```\n\nYou can also use glob patterns to optimize multiple files at once:\n\n```bash\neasy-perms optimize *.yml\n```\n\n## Reviewing Changes\n\nWhile the optimization process is automated, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Review the optimized configuration file to ensure it meets your expectations.\n2. Verify that the permissions are still applied correctly using the `apply` command.\n3. Periodically review and delete the timestamped backup files (e.g., `easy-perms.20230101120000.yml`) once you are satisfied with the changes."},{"id":"controller","title":"Controller and Configuration","body":"> As a matter of convention, I like to create an executable file as  _.\/bin\/perms_ in any application I work on, as the single point of action to assign proper file permissions.\n\n1. First, create the controller file:\n\n   ```shell\n   j a\n   mkdir -p bin\/config\n   touch bin\/apply-perms.sh\n   chmod u+x bin\/apply-perms.sh\n   ```\n3. Paste the controller code (see installation pages) into _bin\/perms_:\n5. Open and edit _bin\/config\/perms.yml_ as necessary. (See how to create on the installation pages).\n\n## Multiple and\/or Local Configuration\n\n1. It is possible to pass multiple configuration paths to the controller. They are processed in the order given, and each later file supersedes the earlier ones. This is how you can have environment-specific overrides, e.g.\n\n   ```bash\n   ... bin\/easy-perms config\/perms.yml config\/perms.local.yml \"$@\"\n   ```\n2. Do not commit _perms.local.yml_\n3. Use it for local-only, additional permissions.\n4. Always list the baseline config first and the environment override last; reversing the order reverses which one wins. See _Multiple Configuration Files_ on the installation pages for the processing details.\n\n## Other Highlights\n\n* Both YAML and JSON is supported.\n* See _json\\_schema\/config.schema.json_ for details of the configuration."},{"id":"drupal","title":"Drupal-Specific Configuration","body":"* [Drupal Best Practices](https:\/\/www.drupal.org\/docs\/administering-a-drupal-site\/security-in-drupal\/securing-file-permissions-and-ownership)\n\n{{ snippet.drupal_yml|fenced }}"},{"id":"readme","title":"Easy Perms","body":"![Banner](..\/..\/images\/easy-perms.jpg)\n\nAdd this to a project to be able to easily manage file and directory permissions.\n\nThe files in a web app are likely to require writable and\/or executable permissions on some files, but not most. Further, these permissions may need to be more permissive in development environments. It's time-consuming and may be confusing to keep all this in order. This tool allows you to set a baseline and then be only as permissive as necessary. It allows different configuration based on an environment. The management is simply to add paths or globs to a YAML or JSON file and then run the controller.\n\n**This project does not handle ownership of files, by design.**  It assumes proper owner and group on all files, and then focuses on setting the correct read, write and execute permissions.\n\n**For security, install this above web root, and not in a location accessibly by the web.**\n\n## Installation (w\/Composer)\n\n1. Installation requires explicit repositories:\n\n   ```shell\n   composer config repositories.9024aba2baa64b08169c0ca94a832bc7 composer https:\/\/packages.intheloftstudios.com\n   composer config repositories.d420c4664ef4513f6bec1e47bd4c5144 github https:\/\/github.com\/aklump\/easy-perms\n   ```\n\n2. Require the latest stable version:\n\n   ```shell\n   composer require aklump\/easy-perms:\">=0.0.28"},{"id":"environments","title":"Environment Strategy","body":"The cleaner model for managing permissions is to **keep the repository in the permission state that is useful for source control and development**, then apply production permissions **after deployment**, on the deployed copy.\n\nIn other words, Git should represent the **source tree**, not the final hardened runtime state.\n\n## Recommended Strategy\n\n1.  **Repository permissions**: Git should store the **source tree** (dev-friendly) permissions.\n2.  **Runtime permissions**: Hardened production permissions should be applied **after deployment** on the production copy.\n\n### Configuration Separation\n\nUse two files to separate hardening from ergonomics:\n\n1.  **`easy-perms.yml`**: Production baseline; strictest permissions.\n2.  **`easy-perms.dev.yml`**: Development overrides; adds executable bits for scripts.\n\nIn development, apply both:\n```shell script\nvendor\/bin\/easy-perms apply easy-perms.yml easy-perms.dev.yml\n```\n\nIn production, apply only the baseline:\n```shell script\nvendor\/bin\/easy-perms apply easy-perms.yml\n```\n\n## Release Workflow\n\n1.  **Develop**: Use dev-friendly executable bits in your checkout.\n2.  **Release**: Commit and tag from a clean working tree.\n3.  **Deploy**: Sync release to production.\n4.  **Harden**: Apply production permissions on the production copy.\n\n### Production Git Working Trees\n\nIf production is a Git checkout, you have two choices:\n*   **Option A**: Set `git config --local core.fileMode false` to ignore mode changes on production.\n*   **Option B**: Deploy to a non-Git release directory (e.g., `releases\/2026-08-03`) and symlink `current` to it.\n\n## Caveat: Timing\n\nApply production hardening only **after** all deployment scripts (e.g., Composer, database updates) have finished. Hardening too early may block your own deployment process."},{"id":"hints","title":"Hints","body":"## Set\/Review Perms by Filename\n\n```shell\n$ bin\/apply-perms.sh -vvv | grep upgrade_npm_deps.sh\nCalculating permissions: **\/upgrade_npm_deps.sh\n0550 \ud83d\udee0  bin\/upgrade_npm_deps.sh\n0550 \ud83d\udee0  install\/composer\/my_module\/knowledge\/vendor\/aklump\/knowledge\/bin\/upgrade_npm_deps.sh\n0550 \ud83d\udee0  install\/composer\/my_theme\/bin\/upgrade_npm_deps.sh\n```"},{"id":"permission_values","title":"What Are the Permission Values?","body":"* See `DefaultFilePermissions` and `DefaultDirectoryPermissions` for default values.\n* To override these default values add something like the following to your configuration:\n\n```yaml\nfile_permissions:\n  default: '0640'\n  readonly: '0440'\n  writable: '0640'\n  executable: '0740'\n\ndirectory_permissions:\n  default: '0750'\n  readonly: '0550'\n  writable: '0770'\n  executable: '0750'\n```\n\n## When Overrides Take Effect\n\nBecause configuration files are processed in order \u2014 each one only after the previous has been fully processed \u2014 an override to `file_permissions` or `directory_permissions` is **not retroactive**. It affects the paths listed in the file that declares it, and any file processed after it.\n\nGiven `easy-perms apply base.yml dev.yml`:\n\n_base.yml_\n\n```yaml\nfile_permissions:\n  writable: '0664'\nwritable:\n  - base.php\n```\n\n_dev.yml_\n\n```yaml\nfile_permissions:\n  writable: '0600'\nwritable:\n  - dev.php\n```\n\nThe result is:\n\n| Path       | Mode   | Why                                                        |\n|------------|--------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| _base.php_ | `0664` | The mode in effect when _base.yml_ was processed.           |\n| _dev.php_  | `0600` | _dev.yml_ raised the override before its own paths ran.     |\n\nTo change the mode of _base.php_ too, either list it again in _dev.yml_ or move the override into _base.yml_."},{"id":"workflow","title":"Workflow Strategy","body":"1. Use `bump dev` when you want to begin developing to get the local perms correct.\n2. Use `bump build` to restore the live permissions before committing to the repo.\n\n## Web Package\n\n1. Set only those execute permissions that are required to `bump build` in _config.yml_. All other configuration for `executable` should be written in _config.local.yml_.\n\n_build\/00\\_set\\_live\\_perms.sh_\n\n```shell\n#!\/usr\/bin\/env bash\n...vendor\/bin\/easy-perms .\/bin\/config\/perms.yml\n```\n\n_dev\/00\\_set\\_dev\\_perms.sh_\n\n* Use _perms.local.yml_ to set the extra executable permissions used in development only.\n\n```shell\n#!\/usr\/bin\/env bash\nchmod u+x ...vendor\/bin\/easy-perms\n...vendor\/bin\/easy-perms .\/bin\/config\/perms.yml .\/bin\/config\/perms.local.yml\n\n```\n\n## Install\/Deployment\n\n1. During deployment to live use `.\/bin\/apply-perms.sh` to ensure the live perms are set correctly.\n4. Better yet, do the last step as part of `.\/bin\/install` by setting something like this:\n\n```yaml\n  pre_install_prod:\n    - cd opt\/aklump\/easy-config && composer install\n  post_install_prod:\n    - bin\/apply-perms.sh\n```\n\n_bin\/perms_\n\n```shell\n#!\/usr\/bin\/env bash\ns=\"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\";[[ \"$s\" ]] || s=\"${(%):-%N}\";while [ -h \"$s\" ];do d=\"$(cd -P \"$(dirname \"$s\")\" && pwd)\";s=\"$(readlink \"$s\")\";[[ $s != \/* ]] && s=\"$d\/$s\";done;__DIR__=$(cd -P \"$(dirname \"$s\")\" && pwd)\n\n$__DIR__\/....vendor\/bin\/easy-perms \"$__DIR__\/config\/perms.yml\" \"$__DIR__\/config\/perms.local.yml\" \"$@\"\n\n```"}]